import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Server Blocks" active="/docs/server/">
    <h1>Server Blocks</h1>
    <p>
        The <code>server</code> keyword starts an HTTP server. It's a top-level
        statement that binds to a port and defines route handlers.
    </p>

    <h2>Syntax</h2>
    <pre><code><span class="tk">server</span> <span class="tn">8080</span> &#123;
    <span class="tc">// route handlers go here</span>
&#125;</code></pre>

    <p>
        The <code>server</code> keyword is followed by a port number and a block
        containing route definitions.
    </p>

    <h2>Behavior</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>The server binds to <code>127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt;</code></li>
        <li>On startup, it prints <code>Harbor server running on http://127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt;</code></li>
        <li>The server uses Hyper and Tokio under the hood for async HTTP</li>
        <li>Unmatched routes return a <code>404 Not Found</code> response</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="note">
        <p>
            <strong>Important:</strong> When the interpreter reaches a
            <code>server</code> statement, it starts the server and blocks. No
            statements after the <code>server</code> block will execute.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Running code before the server</h2>
    <p>
        Place any setup logic — variable assignments, function definitions, print
        statements — before the <code>server</code> block:
    </p>
    <pre><code><span class="tv">version</span> = <span class="ts">"1.0"</span>
<span class="tk">print</span>(<span class="ts">"Starting server..."</span>)

<span class="tk">fn</span> <span class="tv">make_greeting</span>(<span class="tv">name</span>) &#123;
    <span class="ts">"Hello, "</span> + <span class="tv">name</span>
&#125;

<span class="tk">server</span> <span class="tn">3000</span> &#123;
    <span class="tk">get</span> <span class="ts">"/"</span> &#123;
        <span class="tk">respond</span> <span class="tv">make_greeting</span>(<span class="ts">"visitor"</span>)
    &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>

    <h2>Port</h2>
    <p>
        The port must be a number literal. It cannot be a variable or expression.
    </p>
    <pre><code><span class="tc">// ✓ This works</span>
<span class="tk">server</span> <span class="tn">8080</span> &#123; ... &#125;

<span class="tc">// ✗ This does not work</span>
<span class="tv">port</span> = <span class="tn">8080</span>
<span class="tk">server</span> <span class="tv">port</span> &#123; ... &#125;</code></pre>

